   "subject": "Re: [GGIR] GGIR do.impute=1 questions",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>Thanks Dora,<br></div><div><br></div><div>I am afraid I have to disagree with you. Imputing with NA is the same as imputing by the mean of the remaining values. Especially for night time behavior that could be a problematic assumption. So, a real neutral imputation does not exist, even ignoring those data is effectively an imputation.<br></div><div><br></div><div>My advise would be to check the &quot;c<span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">omplete_24hcycle&quot; variable I mentioned. If this value is less than 1 and if the accelerometer has not been worn for at least a couple of days then do not use this recording in your analysis.</span></span></span></span><br></div><div><br></div><div><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">That said if you have ideas on how the imputation approach in GGIR can be improved then you are welcome to work on it and we can add it as an extra option for the user.</span></span></span></span><br></div><div><br></div><div><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Best wishes,</span></span></span></span><br></div><div><br></div><div><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Vincent</span></span></span></span><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>‐‐‐‐‐‐‐ Original Message ‐‐‐‐‐‐‐<br></div>"
    ],
